From: mscharged-port
Subject: New runtime: submit without waiting for the GPU, one staging copy per frame
Upstream-base: d664382f57002fbff143911c81b06b7e7a4b3ab4
Classification: host performance (feat/performance). Apply after 0122.

Development switch of the performance work: RECHARGED_RUNTIME=classic keeps
the path of the earlier patches; without it (or with any other value) the new
runtime runs. The session log names the runtime ("Render runtime: ...").

The classic path waits at every GX draw-done token until the GPU has run the
packet: the game emits its next frame only once the GPU has finished the last
one, so CPU and GPU take turns. By then the parser has copied every source
byte the packet reads, and all later GPU work (the next packet, the VI
present of a display copy) runs after it on the same queue. The new runtime
only submits: the draw-done latches once the packet is submitted and the
previous packet with GPU work has finished (one frame in flight), and a
display copy counts as complete for the VI once it is submitted.

The classic path copies the new staging ranges before every pass (up to four
copies per pass, about 170 per match frame; Mesa's Intel driver spends 2-3 ms
of every frame setting them up). The staging regions only grow within a
native packet, so the new runtime copies each region once, in 16-byte blocks,
in a submission ahead of the packet. Texture uploads keep their place.

A final pass without draws, clears or readers (the empty draw-done packet of
every glx swap) is skipped instead of loading, storing and resolving the EFB.

The GPU commands, their order and the data they read are unchanged.

